# Step-by-step setup for the two integrations that need outside accounts —
# Resend (email) and Stripe Connect (a pilot taking payment from their own
# clients) — is in docs/SETUP.md. This file is the reference for what each
# variable is; that one is the order to do things in.

# Supabase — new, dedicated "master" project. NEVER point this at
# amgaviation/amg1's project (vsynqnqlouvphiniqaiy). See docs/PLAN.md §2.
#
# Note the missing NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix: these names match what's actually
# configured in Vercel. NEXT_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
# are exposed to the browser bundle via the explicit `env` block in
# next.config.ts (Next's supported way to inline a non-NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed
# var), NOT Next's automatic prefix convention. NEXT_SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY
# must never be added to that `env` block — it's read only in
# lib/supabase/service-role.ts, a server-only module.
NEXT_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# Required by the Stripe webhook: provisioning a tenant writes rows for a
# user who has no session in flight (Stripe is calling us, not the pilot),
# so it cannot go through a session-scoped client. Without this the
# webhook fails loudly rather than silently — see service-role.ts.
NEXT_SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# Platform billing — Stripe subscription (we bill the pilot). Separate
# Stripe product/account from Connect below. See docs/PLAN.md §Architecture.
#
# Keep all three in the SAME Stripe mode. The webhook refuses any event
# whose livemode disagrees with the key's mode, so a test event can never
# mutate live data (or vice versa) — but that guard only holds if these
# are not mixed. Use test values until launch.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Three-tier plans (solo / pro / business), monthly and annual — one var
# per Stripe Price object. The NAMES are pinned by lib/entitlements.ts
# (TIER_PRICE_ENV) and tests/entitlements.test.mjs: the webhook maps a
# subscription's price back to its tier through this exact table, so a
# renamed or unset var silently un-maps that tier. Leave a var EMPTY only
# if that tier/interval is genuinely not for sale — the code treats empty
# as unconfigured and the plan pickers show the option as unavailable.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_SOLO=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_SOLO_ANNUAL=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_PRO_ANNUAL=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BUSINESS=
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BUSINESS_ANNUAL=
# Deliberately still absent until per-seat billing is actually built
# (decision #10 defers it): STRIPE_PRICE_ID_BUSINESS_SEAT. Adding an empty
# var now would let "unset" pass for "configured" the day seat-sync lands.
# (The Business TIER above is a flat-rate subscription, not the per-seat
# plan — see the migration 20260812300000_account_plan_tier.sql header.)

# Stripe Connect (Standard) — pilot bills their own client. We never see
# the pilot's keys; this is our platform's Connect application config only.
STRIPE_CONNECT_CLIENT_ID=
# Signing secret for the CONNECTED-ACCOUNTS webhook endpoint — the one that
# records a client's payment onto the invoice automatically
# (app/api/stripe/connect-webhook/route.ts).
#
# THIS IS NOT STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET ABOVE, and copying that value here will
# not work: Stripe mints a distinct whsec_ per endpoint, and the platform
# secret cannot verify a Connect delivery. Two endpoints, two secrets.
#
# To fill it in — dashboard.stripe.com/webhooks, in the SAME mode as
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY (the handler refuses any event whose livemode
# disagrees with the key's):
#   1. Add endpoint, URL https://<your-domain>/api/stripe/connect-webhook
#   2. Choose "Listen to events on connected accounts" — NOT the default,
#      "events on your account". A direct charge on a pilot's own connected
#      account is delivered on the connected-accounts scope only; an
#      endpoint registered the other way receives nothing this route can
#      act on, and the route logs exactly that if it happens.
#   3. Subscribe to THESE FIVE (lib/stripe/connect-payments.ts's
#      CONNECT_ENDPOINT_REGISTER_EVENT_TYPES is the authority; this is a
#      copy, and tests/connect-auto-payment.test.mjs pins the pair):
#        checkout.session.completed
#        checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded
#        checkout.session.async_payment_failed
#        payment_intent.succeeded
#        account.application.deauthorized
#      payment_intent.succeeded (20260817160000) is how an AUTOPAY charge —
#      a recurring invoice charged to a client's saved card — lands on the
#      ledger; without it the money moves and the invoice never shows it.
#      The fourth is a pilot revoking this platform's access from their own
#      Stripe dashboard. Without it that revocation is silent: the account
#      row keeps its connect_account_id, Settings goes on saying Stripe is
#      connected, and link generation fails with an error no retry fixes.
#      The first three, and each earns its place. A link offering a bank payment
#      (ACH) is paid ASYNCHRONOUSLY: `completed` fires at mandate
#      acceptance while payment_status is still 'unpaid' and the money has
#      NOT moved, and one of the other two follows a few business days
#      later. Register only the first and no ACH payment is ever recorded.
#      Register only the first two and a FAILED debit is invisible: the
#      invoice goes on showing "bank payment initiated" for money that is
#      never coming, and the payment link — which Stripe spent at mandate
#      acceptance — is never replaced, so the client cannot try again.
#      THOSE FOUR AND NOTHING ELSE — in particular not charge.refunded and
#      not charge.dispute.*. Money going back out is deliberately never
#      recorded automatically (the argument is in the header of
#      lib/stripe/connect-payments.ts): a pilot who refunds a client in
#      their Stripe dashboard corrects the payment on the invoice
#      themselves. That covers an ACH debit which SETTLES and is disputed
#      afterwards too — Stripe allows that for up to 60 days — since it
#      arrives as a dispute event on this same unsubscribed path.
#      Subscribing to an event this handler does not act on only fills the
#      delivery ledger with 'ignored' rows.
#   4. Copy that endpoint's signing secret into this variable.
#
# LEAVE IT EMPTY AND NOTHING CHANGES. The route refuses every delivery with
# a 503 before touching Stripe or the database, and payments are recorded
# by hand exactly as they were before this feature existed.
STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# TEST-ONLY onboarding bypass. When set, the /welcome plan picker shows a
# hidden, PIN-gated button that creates a COMPED account (no card, no
# Stripe subscription — the stripe_customer_id IS NULL shape docs/BILLING.md
# documents) so onboarding can be walked end to end while testing. Unset
# (the default), the button does not render and the action refuses.
# LEAVE THIS UNSET IN PRODUCTION once real signups start: anyone holding
# the PIN can mint themselves a free account. Use a long random value, not
# four digits. See app/(auth)/welcome/test-bypass-actions.ts.
ONBOARDING_TEST_PIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMAIL — sending an invoice, and chasing it, from inside the product.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both must be set before "Email it to <client>" is offered on an invoice.
# When either is missing the option is hidden entirely and the pilot is told
# to download the PDF and send it themselves — the one thing that must never
# happen is an invoice marked Sent that was never sent (see lib/email/send.ts).
#
# RESEND_API_KEY is the Resend account credential.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# INVOICE_FROM_EMAIL is the sender, e.g. billing@yourdomain.com. Its DOMAIN
# MUST be verified at resend.com/domains (DKIM + SPF) or every send is
# rejected with a 403 "domain is not verified". This is the same verification
# Supabase Auth's confirmation email depends on — an unverified domain breaks
# signup and invoice delivery together, and neither is a code problem.
INVOICE_FROM_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCHEDULED REMINDERS — the one job that sends mail with nobody logged in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shared secret Vercel Cron presents to /api/reminders/run, as
# `Authorization: Bearer <this>`. Generate one with `openssl rand -base64 32`
# and set it as an Environment Variable in the Vercel project (Vercel injects
# the same value into its own cron requests).
#
# THIS IS THE SWITCH. With it unset the route returns 503 and does nothing at
# all — no read, no send, no row — so the `crons` entry in vercel.json is
# inert on any deployment that has not set it. That is deliberate: the entry
# can ship long before the secret does, and a preview deployment cannot chase
# anybody's clients.
#
# Reminders ALSO need RESEND_API_KEY + INVOICE_FROM_EMAIL above (a pass with
# no mail service records nothing and reports what it would have sent), and
# per-client schedules, which are off for every client until a pilot sets one.
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SAMPLE CONNECT INTEGRATION (docs/SAMPLE-CONNECT.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A self-contained DEMO of Stripe Connect with V2 accounts, living under
# lib/sample-connect, app/sample-connect and app/store. It is deliberately
# separate from the PRODUCTION Connect integration configured above:
# different account model (V2 accounts this platform creates, vs Standard
# accounts linked by OAuth), different monetization (an application fee, vs
# none), and its own table for the user->account mapping. It never reads or
# writes pilot.accounts.connect_account_id.
#
# LEAVE THESE UNSET AND THE SAMPLE SIMPLY SAYS SO. Each page renders the
# name of the missing variable instead of its UI, and no route throws.
#
# The sample reuses STRIPE_SECRET_KEY above (same platform, same mode).
#
# The recurring Price a merchant subscribes to — what they pay YOU. Its own
# variable rather than reusing STRIPE_PRICE_ID_* so a demo can never put
# someone on a real plan. A test-mode one already exists for this account:
SAMPLE_CONNECT_PLATFORM_PRICE_ID=price_1U4My3CsUD8TlMqiaidAYrQk
# Signing secret for the SNAPSHOT endpoint (subscription events, "events on
# your account"): /api/stripe/sample-connect/webhook
SAMPLE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Signing secret for the THIN endpoint (V2 account events, "events on
# connected accounts", payload style Thin):
# /api/stripe/sample-connect/webhook-thin
#
# THREE DISTINCT whsec_ VALUES ARE IN PLAY ONCE THE SAMPLE IS RUNNING —
# this one, the one above, and STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET for production billing.
# Stripe mints one per destination and none can verify another's deliveries.
SAMPLE_CONNECT_THIN_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOLD_EXPIRY_PURGE_ENABLED — arms the one unattended job that DELETES data.
#
# The hold-expiry pass (app/api/holds/run) finds accounts whose hold window
# closed unpaid and purges their COMMERCIAL records (never the logbook,
# documents, aircraft or qualifications — that is enforced in the database).
#
# Leave this UNSET. With it unset the pass still runs on its schedule and
# reports exactly which accounts it WOULD have purged, and deletes nothing.
# That dry run against real expiries is the only test that proves the
# selection, because no staging tenant holds a real pilot's records.
#
# Set it to exactly "true" — lower case, nothing else — only after watching
# those dry runs. "TRUE", "1" and "yes" all read as OFF, deliberately.
HOLD_EXPIRY_PURGE_ENABLED=
